    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures). `AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class; `AcceptanceBase` is abstract.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec: return `capacityWarning: true` when the owner's city already holds **40\u201349** owners (approaching the hard limit of 50); the hard rejection at 50 is unchanged.\n\nI mirrored the existing `bulkSignupWarning` feature end-to-end:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `capacityWarning` boolean property to the Owner schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it (and MapStruct maps it automatically by name).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `capacity_warning` column field with getter/setter.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `addOwner`, right after the existing `rejectCityAtCapacity(...)` hard check, set `owner.setCapacityWarning(isApproachingCityCapacity(owner.getCity()))`. The new `isApproachingCityCapacity` method counts existing owners in the city using the same case-insensitive/whitespace-collapsed normalization as the rejection path, and returns true when the count is `>= 40 && < 50`. The `>= 50` rejection runs first and is untouched.\n\n4. **`schema.sql`** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning` column alongside `bulk_signup_warning` in each dialect.\n\nBecause the rejection at 50 runs before the warning computation, a city at capacity is still rejected; a city with 40\u201349 owners flags the next created owner with `capacityWarning: true`, and fewer than 40 leaves it `false`.",
